# The root page of a sample LDES 2.0: the first 10 000 elements of the RiverBench dataset "OfficeGraph"
# (https://w3id.org/riverbench/datasets/officegraph/dev).
#
# RiverBench (https://w3id.org/riverbench) publishes benchmark datasets of RDF streams, in Jelly-RDF among others.
# Every stream element is the set of triples (in the default graph) about one measurement of one of the IoT devices in
# a Dutch office building, and the property that it relates to. The Jelly file that RiverBench hosts (181 KB, 10 000
# stream elements, stream types stax:flatTripleStream, stax:subjectGraphStream) is the one page of this LDES. It has
# no description message, so every message in the file is a member message: one Jelly frame is one member.
#
# How this maps to LDES (everything RiverBench says about the dataset itself is in ../catalog.ttl):
#  * membership: every message has exactly one subject of type saref:Measurement, with an IRI of its own: that is the
#    member identifier. The other triples of the message (about the measured property) belong to the member.
#  * ordering: RiverBench states that the elements are ordered from oldest to newest by saref:hasTimestamp (its
#    rb:hasTemporalProperty says sosa:resultTime, which this data does not use). The values are in ascending order in
#    the file.
#    These xsd:dateTime values have no timezone. A client then compares them as if they were in the same, unknown,
#    timezone, which is exact within this LDES. The timezone is not stated by RiverBench, so no ldes:defaultTimezone
#    is claimed here. If it is known, e.g. "+01:00", add it to the LDES for clients that mix these timestamps with
#    timestamps that have a timezone.
#  * ldes:shape: the shape that every member conforms to (checked on all members by the generator).
#  * ldes:immutable: the Jelly file never changes, so a client that has read it does not fetch it again.
#  * the file has 10 000 messages and 10 000 distinct member identifiers, as RiverBench states
#    (rb:hasStreamElementCount). This was checked when this file was generated.
#
# Try it:  npx ldes-client https://ldes.tech/riverbench/officegraph/root.trig
# Generated by scripts/generate-riverbench.mjs of the ldes.tech repository; do not edit by hand.
#
@prefix ldes: <https://w3id.org/ldes#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix saref: <https://saref.etsi.org/core/> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

# The LDES: the IRI of this event stream is the root page, with a different fragment identifier
<#EventStream> a ldes:EventStream ;
    ldes:memberType saref:Measurement ;
    ldes:timestampPath saref:hasTimestamp ;
    ldes:shape <#MemberShape> .

# The focus node of this shape is the member identifier
<#MemberShape> a sh:NodeShape ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path rdf:type ;
        sh:hasValue saref:Measurement ;
        sh:minCount 1
    ] ;
    sh:property [
        sh:path saref:hasTimestamp ;
        sh:datatype xsd:dateTime ;
        sh:minCount 1 ;
        sh:maxCount 1
    ] .

# The root page itself. It has a relation to the page with all the members of this LDES.
# The interval is that of the first and last timestamp in the file (the upper bound is exclusive).
<> ldes:relation [
    ldes:page <https://w3id.org/riverbench/datasets/officegraph/dev/files/jelly_10K.jelly.gz> ;
    ldes:timeFrom "2022-02-28T23:59:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ldes:timeUntil "2022-03-01T05:47:01"^^xsd:dateTime
] .

# A page can be marked immutable by the page that links to it, before it is fetched.
# This is stated about the linked page, not about the relation.
<https://w3id.org/riverbench/datasets/officegraph/dev/files/jelly_10K.jelly.gz> ldes:immutable true .
